===Pronunciation===
[[File:Aphrodite with turtle-AO 20126-IMG 4347-gradient.jpg|thumb|A 2nd- to 3rd-century [[statue]] of {{w|Aphrodite Urania}},{{refn|group=n|name=n1|From the Collection of the {{w|Louvre}} in [[Paris]], [[France]].}} depicted standing on a [[tortoise]] which was a [[symbol]] of [[domestic#Adjective|domestic]] [[modesty]] and [[chastity]]. Aphrodite Urania [[signify|signified]] the [[heavenly]] [[aspect]] of the [[Greek#Adjective|Greek]] [[goddess]] [[Aphrodite]], and gave rise to the word ''Uranian'' meaning “of Aphrodite Urania [...]: heavenly, [[spiritual]], as [[contrast#Verb|contrasted]] with the [[earthly]] aspect of {{w|Aphrodite Pandemos}}” ''(sense 3)'', as well as “[[homosexual#Adjective|homosexual]]” ''(sense 2)''.]]

* {{a|RP}} {{IPA|en|/jʊˈɹeɪnɪən/}}
* {{a|GA}} {{IPA|en|/jʊˈɹeɪniən/}}
* {{rhymes|en|eɪniən}}
* {{hyphenation|en|Ura|ni|an}}

===Etymology 1===
{{PIE root|en|h₁wers}}
From {{der|en|la|Ūrania||muse of astronomy in Greek mythology}} {{suffix|en||an|pos2=''suffix forming {{glossary|agent noun}}s''}}. ''Ūrania'' is derived from {{der|en|grc|Οὐρᾰνῐ́ᾱ||muse of astronomy}}, from {{m|grc|οὐράνιος||of or relating to the sky, celestial, heavenly}} (from {{m|grc|οὐρανός||the sky; heaven, home of the gods; the universe}}, probably ultimately from {{der|en|ine-pro|*h₁wers-||rain}}) + {{m|grc|-ιος|pos=''suffix forming adjectives meaning ‘pertaining to’''}}.<ref name="OED">{{R:OED Online|pos=''adj.{{sup|1}}'' and ''n.{{sup|2}}''|noformat=yes|id=220339|date=March 2012|nodot=yes}}; {{R:Lexico|alt=Uranian{{sup|1}}|pos=adj.'' and ''n}}</ref><ref>{{R:OED Online|entry=Urania|pos=n|id=220338|date=March 2012}}</ref>

Adjective sense 2 (“homosexual”) and the noun sense (“a homosexual”) refer to {{w|Plato}}’s work ''[[w:Symposium (Plato)|Symposium]]'' ({{circa2|385–370 {{B.C.E.|dots=on}}|short=yes}}), where the [[goddess]] [[Aphrodite]], in her [[heavenly]] [[aspect]] {{w|Aphrodite Urania}} ''(see adjective sense 3)'' is described as inspiring a [[noble#Adjective|noble]] form of affection between older and younger men.<ref name="OED"/> Compare {{cog|de|Urning||a homosexual, Uranian}}, {{m|de|Urnigtum||homosexuality}}, also referring to Aphrodite Urania, coined by the German writer {{w|Karl Heinrich Ulrichs}} (1825–1895) in 1864. By the 1900s, the use of the word in this sense had largely been supplanted by {{m|en|homosexual}} ''(see further at that entry)''.

