#: {{synonyms|en|uranistic|q1=obsolete|Thesaurus:homosexual}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Arthur Lyon Raile [pseudonym; {{w|Edward Perry Warren}}]|chapter=The Uranian Idea|title=A Defence of Uranian Love|volume_plain=part the second (The Uranian Eros)|location=[London]|publisher=Privately printed|year=1930|pages=19–20|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=BJETAAAAQAAJ&pg=PA19|oclc=156094194|passage=His &#91;{{w|Plato}}'s&#93; Philosophical Eros is not the '''Uranian''' Eros which possessed the nation, and the '''Uranian''' doctrine is not primarily either philosophical or paederastic. It is a value attached to the masculine which included philosophy as masculine and paedersaty when not anti-masculine, but was itself the idea and form of virtue.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Linda [C.] Dowling|chapter=The Higher Sodomy|title=Hellenism and Homosexuality in Victorian Oxford|location=Ithaca, N.Y.|publisher={{w|Cornell University Press}}|year=1994|page=134|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=YFT_7wfus0QC&pg=PA134|isbn=978-0-8014-2960-6|passage=Before these ideals would once again become available to "homosexuality" as a positive social identity, however, the '''Uranian''' modality of male love would be rejected by the avant-garde itself as an outworn fashion.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Chuck Stewart|chapter=Biographical Sketches|title=Gay and Lesbian Issues: A Reference Handbook|series=Contemporary World Issues|location=Santa Barbara, Calif.|publisher={{w|ABC-CLIO}}|year=2003|page=138|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=sdKvt-GoidYC&pg=PA138|isbn=978-1-85109-372-4|passage=[[w:Edward Carpenter|[Edward] Carpenter]] was disappointed by these meetings with [[w:Walt Whitman|[Walt] Whitman]] but began to integrate a positive homosexual view into his socialist writings. Carpenter saw the '''Uranian''' (homosexual) spirit to be more enlightened than that of the common man and believed that Uranians were the new prophets of the coming social revolution.}}
# {{lb|en|not comparable|Greek mythology|Roman mythology}} Of {{w|Aphrodite Urania}}, the heavenly [[aspect]] of [[Greek#Adjective|Greek]] [[goddess]] of [[beauty]] and [[love#Noun|love]] [[Aphrodite]] and her [[Roman#Adjective|Roman]] [[counterpart]] [[Venus]]: heavenly, [[spiritual]], as [[contrast#Verb|contrasted]] with the [[earthly]] aspect of {{w|Aphrodite Pandemos}}.
#: {{antonyms|en|pandemian|pandemic}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Percy Bysshe Shelley|authorlink=Percy Bysshe Shelley|chapter=[[w:Symposium (Plato)|The Banquet]]. Translated from {{w|Plato}}.|editor=Mrs. Shelley [''i.e.'', {{w|Mary Shelley}}]|title=Essays, Letters from Abroad, Translations and Fragments, [...] In Two Volumes|volume=I|location=Philadelphia, Pa.|publisher=Lea and Blanchard|year=1840|page=86|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=_aIIAAAAQAAJ&pg=PA86|oclc=1045107129|passage=For assuredly are there two Venuses; one, the eldest, the daughter of Uranus, born without a mother, whom we call the '''Uranian'''; the other younger, the daughter of Jupiter and Dione, whom we call the Pandemian;—of necessity must there also be two Loves, the '''Uranian''' and Pandemian companions of these goddesses. [...] [T]he attendant on the other, the '''Uranian''', whose nature is entirely masculine, is the Love who inspires us with affection, and exempts us from all wantonness and libertinism.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Steven Berg|chapter=Pausanias: Noble Lies and the Fulfillment of Greekness|title=Eros and the Intoxications of Enlightenment: On Plato’s Symposium|series=SUNY Series in Ancient Greek Philosophy|location=Albany, N.Y.|publisher=[[w:State University of New York|State University of New York Press]]|year=2010|section=part 1 (Athens and Enlightenment)|page=27|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=KHjaGM7gYKAC&pg=PA27|isbn=978-1-4384-3017-1|passage=That this is indeed the general tendency of his &#91;{{w|Pausanias of Athens}}'&#93; speech is made clear by two remarks that follow immediately upon his differentiating the Pandemian from the '''Uranian''' gods. [...] But what is true for Aphrodite seems to be a contradiction in terms: she is both one and two and all gods must be praised—both '''Uranian''' and Pandemian—and yet only the '''Uranian''' ought to be praised [...].}}
# {{lb|en|not comparable|Greek mythology}} Relating to [[Urania]], the [[muse#Noun|muse]] of [[astronomy]].
# {{lb|en|not comparable|by extension|historical|rare}} Of or pertaining to astronomy; [[astronomical]].
