# {{lb|en|chiefly|science fiction}} An [[inhabitant]] of the [[planet]] [[Uranus]]. {{defdate|from 1830s}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Richard A[nthony] Proctor|authorlink=Richard A. Proctor|chapter=Uranus and Neptune, the Arctic Planets|title=Other Worlds than Ours: The Plurality of Worlds Studied under the Light of Recent Scientific Researches|edition=3rd|location=London|publisher=[[w:Longman|Longmans, Green, and Co.]]|year=1872|pages=171–172|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=6GDSAAAAMAAJ&pg=PA171|oclc=16504256|passage=And obviously, since the year of the '''Uranians''' lasts 84 of our years, the continuance of the sun above the horizon would last for many years. [...] And as with the summer day, so with the winter night, years elapse before either comes to an end. For upwards of 20 years, in a latitude corresponding to that of London, the '''Uranians'''—if there are any—never see the small Uranian sun.}}

