# {{lb|en|astronomy}} Of or pertaining to the [[planet]] [[Uranus]]. {{defdate|from 1830s}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|title=The Planets: Are They Inhabited Worlds?|editor={{w|Dionysius Lardner}}|magazine=The Museum of Science & Art|location=London|publisher=Walton and Maberly,{{nb...|Upper Gower Street, and Ivy Lane, Paternoster Row.}}|year=1854|volume=I|issue=6|section=chapter III, paragraph 8|page=36|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=Lot1H1tmMeQC&pg=PA36|oclc=83638034|passage=The '''Uranian''' year is equal to 84 terrestrial years, or 30687 terrestrial days; and the '''Uranian''' day, according to the probable estimate, being shorter than the terrestrial day in the ration of 1 to 2{{frac|526|1000}}, it follows that the '''Uranian''' year consists of 77336 '''Uranian''' days.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Richard A[nthony] Proctor|authorlink=Richard A. Proctor|chapter=Uranus and Neptune, the Arctic Planets|title=Other Worlds than Ours: The Plurality of Worlds Studied under the Light of Recent Scientific Researches|edition=3rd|location=London|publisher=[[w:Longman|Longmans, Green, and Co.]]|year=1872|page=170|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=6GDSAAAAMAAJ&pg=PA170|oclc=16504256|passage=The inclination of the plane of Uranus's equator to the path in which he travels being about 76°, it follows that the '''Uranian''' sun has a range of about 76° on either side of the celestial equator, during the long '''Uranian''' year.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=John Gunther|authorlink=John Gunther|chapter=Foreword|title=[[w:Death Be Not Proud (book)|Death Be Not Proud: A Memoir]]|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher=[[w:Harper (publisher)|Harper & Brothers]]|year=1949|oclc=491067405|edition2=Harper Perennial Modern Classics|location2=New York, N.Y.|publisher2={{w|Harper Perennial}}|year2=2007|page2=9|pageurl2=https://books.google.com/books?id=ZZ0phPPfy6cC&pg=PA9|isbn2=978-0-06-123097-4|passage=He had not quite decided whether to be a physicist or a chemist; probably he would have chosen the trans-'''Uranian''' borderland between the two.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=A. M. Fridman|author2=N[ick] N. Gor′kavyi|title=The Dynamics of Planetary Rings and the Prediction of New Uranian Satellites|editor=[[w:Isaak Markovich Khalatnikov|I[saac] M[arkovich] Khalatnikov]]|journal=Physics Reviews|series=Soviet Scientific Reviews, Section A|location=London|publisher=Harwood Academic Publishers|year=1989|volume=12, part 4|page=326|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=oclj9lNI9QAC&pg=PA326|isbn=978-3-7186-4930-3|passage=A decisive argument supporting this hypothesis was the discovery of a remarkable characteristic of the arrangement of the '''Uranian''' rings: near the outer edge of the rings there exist several orbits each of which is in resonance with a pair of rings simultaneously.}}

